WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.150
Film Courage: Can we go back to the graph
for a moment and see the intensity?

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.870
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: Sure [holds
up the graph] this shows you what you’re

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.889
doing.

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:14.339
You’re writing little short sentences and
you’re putting hyphens and then you’re

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:16.010
putting a line connecting them all.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:21.270
But then when you put it on the side you can
see the shape of your story and you can see

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.780
where it needs some attention.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:31.910
There are all these peaks here but no real
valleys but it would be much more dramatic

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.400
if you removed some of the intensity or you
removed or you added less intense scenes in

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.060
here so that the rises would be greater.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.710
And it could be that everything is just fine
when you do this and it looks really perfect

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.660
but most of the time you’ll discover that
it’s not a roller coaster ride which you

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.980
want your reader to go on.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.620
You want them to be screaming all the time
basically.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.230
Film Courage: And then toward the end you
see the highest peak and then it levels down.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:06.460
Ken: Yes, it levels down and in today’s
storytelling world maybe this is not the right

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.490
way to end a story.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.950
It might be better to end on a higher peak.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.090
Film Courage: That is JAWS?

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:18.960
Ken: No this is just a made up story that
we use as an example.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:26.370
That’s just like Clyde Millie outlined the
GRAPES OF WRATH, you sit down with a movie

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:32.870
like JAWS or THE MEG that’s coming out in
August from Warner Brothers and chart it and

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:39.570
you’ll see how conscious the story is of
these ups and downs.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.590
People, they know what they’re doing.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.570
Directors are known for their ability to do
that.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:54.100
If you want a crazy all out screaming ride,
go see LAURA CROFT or one of the JAMES BOND

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.950
movies and you’ll see that this is what
they try and deliver to you.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.479
And if you want a more tempered ride where
you can get deeper into the story because

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:09.140
you have a moment to rest between peaks, then
you’ll see another kind of story.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.470
Film Courage: You were saying that today’s
peaks might end a little higher?

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.010
Would that be because there might be a possibility
of a sequel or…

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:25.890
Ken: Yes, usually it’s that and it’s also
because ever since them moments that STAR

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.650
WARS hit the screens, I’ll never forget
that moment because when I watched that movie

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.520
I thought “This is a watershed in the history
of movies.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.300
We will never look at movies the same way
again,” because the scenes were the shortest

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.099
scenes I’ve ever seen.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:45.380
Scenes before that probably averaged two to
three minutes but in STAR WARS the scenes

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.830
seemed like they lasted 6 seconds.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:54.420
For 10 seconds and you could not see everything
in the seen which made you instantly fall

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.660
in love with the movie because you wouldn’t
believe in the world because it was so chocked

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.870
full of stuff you couldn’t see it all, you
just have to see it again.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.500
And I thought “This is brilliant.”

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.090
And it was a foreshadowing of the attention
span that we are now fully living with.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.510
We weren’t quite there yet when it came
out.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.060
It was a little bit ahead of its time.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:21.170
But it totally predicted the world we live
in now where our attention span is just minute

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:26.650
because we’re being bombarded by so many
pieces of information from so many directions.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:33.500
We are distracted all the time and…the text
is ringing…the phone is ringing…the email

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:38.129
is ringing…are head is ringing…our eyes
are buzzing.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:44.900
Somebody said Americans look at 52,000 commercials
every day in a normal day and I think that’s

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:45.900
true.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.319
I mean assuming you commute to work and you’re
looking at everything out there, busses going

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:52.770
by, billboards, etc.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:04:00.650
So I think there is another example of a filmmaker
who understood the audience psychology and

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:06.830
who directly addressed it, who directly addressed
that that is what he’s all about grabbing

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.750
your psychology and playing with it and you
love it because nobody has done that to you

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.160
before.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.949
Nobody was making movies that you had to immediately
go see again because you had to see what that

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:22.860
little Gizmo was in the far corner that you
didn’t happen to focus on and now the scene

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.180
is over and you’re on to another scene and
you missed that, so you’ve got to go back

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.809
to train your eye to watch for that corner
and…you know how that is you’re always

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:35.930
thinking “Next time I watch it I’m going
to watch this corner or this corner because

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.650
I know I don’t have the chance to see it
all.”

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.220
And that is really screwing with the psychology
of the audience…love that.

